<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/intermediate/people-and-organisations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Organisations are <strong>systems of people coordinated toward outcomes</strong> — and
    a large share of business failures attributed to "strategy" or "the market" are
    actually coordination, incentive or hiring failures wearing a disguise. The craft
    this page teaches has four levers, matching the syllabus: <em>motivate</em> people
    with more than money, <em>organise</em> them so information flows to decisions,
    <em>recruit and train</em> with methods that actually predict performance, and
    <em>communicate</em> deliberately — because an organisation is, at bottom, a
    decision-making machine whose inputs are conversations.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="hiring, incentives, motivation, culture — the four levers" widget="equationStep" config={peopleCfg} />

  <h2>Motivating employees</h2>
  <p>
    The century of motivation research tells one long story: money matters, and it is
    not enough. Frederick Taylor's <strong>scientific management</strong> (1911) treated
    workers as output-maximisers to be paid by the piece — it raised productivity and
    treated humans as parts, and modern piece-rate systems still inherit both its power
    and its pathologies. Abraham Maslow's <strong>hierarchy of needs</strong> proposed
    that people ascend a ladder — physiological needs, safety, belonging, esteem,
    self-actualisation — and that a satisfied need stops motivating; crude as a science,
    durable as a management insight (a pay rise stops mattering quickly; growth and
    recognition keep working). Frederick Herzberg's <strong>two-factor theory</strong>
    made the sharpest operational cut: <em>hygiene factors</em> (pay, conditions,
    policies, job security) prevent dissatisfaction but generate none; <em>motivators</em>
    (achievement, recognition, responsibility, growth) generate satisfaction but can't
    compensate for failed hygiene. The practical consequence: pay competitively — enough
    that money leaves the negotiation — then differentiate with the work itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    The strongest modern evidence comes from <strong>self-determination theory</strong>
    (Deci and Ryan), popularised by Daniel Pink as <strong>autonomy, mastery and
    purpose</strong>: people do their best work when they direct their own tasks, see
    themselves getting better at something that matters. Its darker companion is
    <em>crowding-out</em>: extrinsic rewards can destroy intrinsic motivation. Uri
    Gneezy and Aldo Rustichini's field study at Haifa day-care centres is the canonical
    case: introducing a small fine for late child-pickup <em>increased</em> lateness —
    the fine converted a moral obligation into a purchasable price. And incentives that
    work too well produce their own disasters: Wells Fargo's sales quotas drove employees
    to open roughly 3.5 million unauthorised accounts (revealed 2016), exactly as
    measured — the metric, not the intent, was rewarded. Design principle: reward the
    outcome you want, audit every outcome you didn't list, and remember Goodhart's law —
    when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> motivation is perks. Perks are hygiene with branding.
    The levers that survive scrutiny — fair pay, real autonomy, visible mastery,
    worthwhile purpose, recognition tied to genuine achievement — are mostly
    <em>design decisions about the work</em>, not additions to it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two further frameworks earn their place on a manager's shelf. <strong>Vroom's
    expectancy theory</strong> models motivation as a product: effort × expectancy
    ("can I do it?") × instrumentality ("will it be noticed?") × valence ("do I want
    the reward?") — and because it multiplies, a zero anywhere zeroes the whole. The
    unmotivated employee usually isn't lazy; one of the three links is broken — the
    task feels impossible, the reward never arrives, or the reward is worthless to
    them. Diagnosis beats exhortation. <strong>Job design</strong> (Hackman and
    Oldham's job characteristics model) specifies the properties of work that create
    internal motivation: <em>skill variety</em> (using different abilities),
    <em>task identity</em> (completing a whole, visible piece), <em>task
    significance</em> (impact on others), <em>autonomy</em> (discretion over how) and
    <em>feedback</em> (seeing the result). Jobs scoring high on these produce the
    psychological states — meaningfulness, responsibility, knowledge of results — that
    sustain performance without supervision. This is why the same person can be
    listless in one role and ferocious in another: the work, not the worker, changed.
  </p>
  <p>
    The theory cashes out as a menu of non-financial redesigns, each targeting a
    different deficit: <em>job rotation</em> (swap between tasks — variety without
    changed responsibility), <em>job enlargement</em> (more tasks at the same level —
    variety, sometimes just more of the same boredom), <em>job enrichment</em> (add
    planning, deciding and checking — the vertical load that raises autonomy and
    responsibility), and <em>teamworking</em> (whole, visible outputs plus belonging).
    Financial methods sit alongside: piece rates, bonuses, profit-sharing, share
    options, fringe benefits — each correct for its context and corrosive outside it
    (piece rates where quality matters more than quantity; bonuses that become
    entitlements). The craft is matching the mechanism to the deficit the diagnosis
    found, not applying the fashionable one.
  </p>

  <h2>Organisation and management</h2>
  <p>
    Structure is the organisation's answer to "who decides what, with whose information?".
    An <strong>organisational chart</strong> shows the hierarchy: layers (<em>tall</em>
    structures have many, <em>flat</em> ones few) and <strong>span of control</strong>
    (how many people report to each manager). Every added layer filters and delays
    information — classic bureaucracies become rumour engines precisely because formal
    channels are slow — while every widened span stretches a manager's attention.
    <strong>Centralised</strong> decisions (made at the top) are consistent and fast in a
    crisis; <strong>decentralised</strong> ones (pushed to the edge) respond to local
    reality and develop managers — most real firms oscillate. <strong>Delegation</strong>
    — passing authority down with the responsibility to match — is the only mechanism by
    which leaders scale; delegating work without authority just creates frustrated
    errand-runners. Management style sits on a spectrum from <em>autocratic</em> (decide
    and instruct — right in fires, corrosive as a steady state), through
    <em>democratic</em> (consult, then decide — slower, better adopted), to
    <em>laissez-faire</em> (set the goal, get out of the way — brilliant with expert
    self-starters, abdication with juniors). The under-taught law of this section is
    <strong>Conway's law</strong> (1968): organisations design systems that mirror their
    communication structures — ship four teams, get a product with four seams. The
    deliberate corollary, the "inverse Conway manoeuvre": design the teams to match the
    architecture you want, because restructuring the org chart is often the only way to
    restructure the product.
  </p>
  <p>
    Charles Handy's culture typology adds vocabulary for what the chart can't show:
    <em>role culture</em> (the Greek temple — rules and procedures rule, classic
    bureaucracies), <em>task culture</em> (the net — teams assembled around projects,
    where expertise outranks rank), <em>person culture</em> (the cluster — the
    organisation serves the members, as in partnerships and practices) and
    <em>power culture</em> (the web — everything radiates from a centre, fast but
    fragile beyond the founder's reach). Mismatches explain a familiar misery: the
    startup's task culture strangled by acquirer's role culture loses exactly the
    people it bought.
  </p>

  <h2>Recruitment, selection and training</h2>
  <p>
    Hiring is the highest-leverage decision most managers make: a great hire multiplies
    a team for years; a bad one consumes a year of management attention and drags
    standards for everyone who watches the cost be tolerated. <strong>Recruitment</strong>
    starts with a real job analysis: what will this person actually do? Internal
    promotion (cheap, motivating, keeps knowledge — but narrow and sometimes just
    reshuffles a gap) versus external hiring (fresh skills and perspective — slower,
    riskier, more expensive). <strong>Selection</strong> is where the evidence is
    unambiguous. Decades of personnel-psychology meta-analysis (Schmidt and Hunter's
    programme of work) rank predictors by how well they forecast job performance:
    <strong>work samples</strong> (do a slice of the actual job) and
    <strong>structured interviews</strong> (same questions, scored rubrics, focused on
    past behaviour — "tell me about a time when…") are the top performers;
    <strong>unstructured conversations</strong> predict charm and similarity, not
    performance; years of experience and unproctored references barely beat chance.
    The reason is signal quality: structure strips the noise — interviewer mood,
    rapport, halo effects — that unstructured formats mistake for signal. A panel that
    can't explain what its rubric was measuring has usually measured nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Training</strong> then takes over: <em>induction</em> (the first weeks, when
    most avoidable attrition happens), <em>on-the-job</em> training (cheap, contextual,
    riskier for customers and mentors), <em>off-the-job</em> (courses, simulations —
    cleaner but transfers weakly back to the work), and the form modern firms treat as
    most valuable: deliberate, feedback-rich practice on real work. Note what training
    signals as much as what it teaches — firms that develop people recruit better people,
    a loop worth more than the course fees. The economics reinforce it: replacing a
    departing employee costs a substantial fraction of their annual salary once
    recruitment, ramp-up and lost knowledge are counted, which is why retention through
    development usually beats hiring as an investment — and why "we train them and they
    might leave" is answered by "what if you don't train them and they stay?"
  </p>

  <h2>Internal and external communication</h2>
  <p>
    Communication is the organisation's bloodstream; its blockages mimic disease.
    <strong>Internal</strong> communication runs <em>vertical</em> (downward: strategy,
    decisions; upward: reality, warnings — the direction most organisations starve,
    because bad news must climb a hierarchy that punishes messengers),
    <em>horizontal</em> (across departments, where silos breed duplication and blame)
    and via the informal <em>grapevine</em> (faster than official channels, inaccurate in
    exact proportion to how little official communication exists). <strong>External</strong>
    communication — with customers, suppliers, investors, regulators, the public — is
    the firm's face, and its failures are expensive in trust rather than time. The
    classic <strong>barriers</strong> all have operational fixes: jargon (write for the
    reader's vocabulary, not your own), channel mismatch (sensitive news in person, not
    email; complex nuance in writing, not a meeting), overload (ten memos teach nobody),
    and the absence of feedback loops (communication isn't complete when the message is
    sent; it's complete when the receiver's understanding is verified). Distributed
    organisations raise the stakes: when presence can't substitute for clarity,
    <em>writing</em> becomes the core management skill — the organisations that thrived
    remotely redesigned rituals around documentation, not just relocated them.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The quantitative layer. <strong>Coordination cost grows quadratically</strong>: a
    team of n people has n(n−1)/2 communication paths — five people, ten links; twenty
    people, 190. This single formula explains why small teams outperform committees
    (Amazon's "two-pizza teams" institutionalise it), why "just add more people" slows
    projects (Brooks's law: adding manpower to a late software project makes it later —
    each addition consumes senior attention while ramping), and why reorganisations
    around mission-sized teams keep beating reorganisations around skills. <strong>Culture
    is the compression of all of the above</strong>: not the values poster but the sum of
    who gets hired, fired, rewarded and tolerated — the programme that runs when nobody
    is watching. Amy Edmondson's research on <strong>psychological safety</strong> — the
    shared belief that questions, admissions and dissent are safe — found it the
    strongest predictor of team performance in Google's Project Aristotle (reported
    2016), and the mechanism is visible everywhere: teams without it hide errors, and
    hidden errors compound. Aviation learned this at the cost of crashes (crew resource
    management explicitly trains juniors to challenge captains); medicine and software
    are mid-adoption. The open questions are live: how incentive design survives AI
    colleagues that optimise measured metrics better than any human; whether flat
    structures scale at all past Dunbar-scale groups; and what happens to organisational
    learning when the apprenticeship-by-presence model of training meets a workforce
    that is partly remote and partly synthetic.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brooks, The Mythical Man-Month; Pink, Drive; Edmondson, The Fearless Organization; Duhigg, "What Google Learned From Its Quest" (NYT, 2016); Gneezy &amp; Rustichini, "A Fine Is a Price" (2000); Schmidt &amp; Hunter (1998) on selection validity.</p>
</TopicPage>
